## Steps

### Step 1: Compare two query results (before/after)

Run the same query pack against two databases or two snapshots and diff the SARIF output. The diff is the comparison — added findings are new, removed findings are gone, and unchanged findings confirm stability.

```bash
codeql database run-deletes codeql-db --queries=custom-pack \
  --output=before.sarif
git checkout release-tag
codeql database run-deletes codeql-db --queries=custom-pack \
  --output=after.sarif
diff <(jq -S .before.sarif) <(jq -S .after.sarif)
```

The first run captures the current state; the second run against the tagged snapshot captures the baseline. Sorting both JSON files with `jq -S` makes the diff meaningful regardless of result ordering.

<code_cell>
# last_verified: 2026-10-11 · codeql n/a
# Model the before/after comparison as data: runs, outputs, and the diff surface
BEFORE_AFTER = {
    "runs": 2,
    "inputs": ["current HEAD database", "tagged snapshot database"],
    "output": "before.sarif + after.sarif",
    "diff_tool": "jq -S on both, then diff",
    "what_the_diff_shows": "added findings, removed findings, unchanged findings",
    "control": "operator picks the two snapshots and runs each pack once",
}
for k, v in BEFORE_AFTER.items():
    print(f"{k:<22}: {v}")
</code_cell>

### Step 2: Compare two scan modes

Run the same query pack once in CLI mode and once in Actions mode and compare the result sets. The comparison answers whether the two modes agree, not whether either is right.

```bash
# CLI mode: results stay local
codeql database analyze codeql-db --queries=custom-pack \
  --format=sarif-latest --output=cli-results.sarif

# Actions mode: results upload as repository alerts
# (run the companion workflow, then read the alerts via the API)
```

The CLI run produces a local SARIF file you can diff directly. The Actions run produces repository alerts; read them back through the GitHub API to build the same SARIF-shaped input for the diff.

<code_cell>
# last_verified: 2026-10-11 · codeql n/a
# Model the scan-mode comparison as data
SCAN_MODES = {
    "cli": {
        "command": "codeql database analyze codeql-db --queries=custom-pack --format=sarif-latest --output=cli-results.sarif",
        "result_sink": "local SARIF file",
        "control": "operator owns each invocation and flag",
    },
    "actions": {
        "command": "init, autobuild, analyze steps in a workflow file",
        "result_sink": "repository code-scanning alerts",
        "control": "workflow file owns the sequence; runner owns execution",
    },
}
for mode, info in SCAN_MODES.items():
    print(f"{mode:<8}: sink={info['result_sink']:<32} control={info['control']}")
</code_cell>

### Step 3: Compare two data-flow configurations

A single query can carry two `TaintTracking::Configuration` variants — for example, local flow only versus global flow. Comparing them answers whether the stricter configuration catches more, and whether the looser one adds noise.

```ql
class LocalFlowConfig extends TaintTracking::Configuration {
  LocalFlowConfig() { this = "LocalFlowConfig" }
  override predicate isSource(DataFlow::Node source) { source instanceof StringLiteralSource }
  override predicate isSink(DataFlow::Node sink) { sink instanceof CredentialSink }
}

class GlobalFlowConfig extends TaintTracking::Configuration {
  GlobalFlowConfig() { this = "GlobalFlowConfig" }
  override predicate isSource(DataFlow::Node source) { source instanceof StringLiteralSource }
  override predicate isSink(DataFlow::Node sink) { sink instanceof CredentialSink }
  override predicate isBarrier(DataFlow::Node node) { node instanceof SanitizingCall }
}
```

The two configurations share the source and sink classes but differ in barriers. Run both and diff the finding sets; findings present in global but absent in local are the ones the barrier was hiding.
<code_cell>
# last_verified: 2026-10-11 · codeql n/a
# Model the two data-flow configurations as data
DATAFLOW_CONFIGS = {
    "local": {
        "barriers": "none beyond the sink",
        "catches": "direct assignments and same-function flows",
        "noise_risk": "low",
    },
    "global": {
        "barriers": "SanitizingCall and module boundaries",
        "catches": "flows that cross function boundaries",
        "noise_risk": "medium to high",
    },
}
for cfg, info in DATAFLOW_CONFIGS.items():
    print(f"{cfg:<8}: barriers={info['barriers']:<42} noise={info['noise_risk']}")
print("Findings in global but not local = flows the barrier was hiding.")
</code_cell>

### Step 4: Compare a custom query against the default suite

Run the default suite and the custom pack separately against the same database, then diff. Findings present in both are redundant; findings present only in the custom pack are net-new coverage.

```bash
codeql database analyze codeql-db --format=sarif-latest --output=default.sarif
codeql database analyze codeql-db --queries=custom-pack \
  --format=sarif-latest --output=custom.sarif
diff <(jq -S .default.sarif) <(jq -S .custom.sarif)
```

A custom query that produces only findings the default suite already produces has not added coverage. A custom query that produces findings the default suite does not is worth keeping — but confirm those findings are real before wiring the query into CI.

<code_cell>
# last_verified: 2026-10-11 · codeql n/a
# Model the custom-vs-default comparison as data
CUSTOM_DEFAULT = {
    "runs": 2,
    "inputs": ["default suite", "custom pack"],
    "output": "default.sarif + custom.sarif",
    "diff_tool": "jq -S on both, then diff",
    "what_the_diff_shows": "redundant findings (in both), net-new findings (custom only), missed findings (default only)",
    "control": "same database, same CLI invocation shape",
}
for k, v in CUSTOM_DEFAULT.items():
    print(f"{k:<22}: {v}")
</code_cell>

